// HQ_3 / OQ_3 / NQ_3: hard MCQ, open questions and extra normal MCQ for scott, davoudi, alex, rc
const HQ_3=[];const OQ_3=[];const NQ_3=[];

// ===== scott =====
HQ_3.push(
{"t":"scott","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Le Corbusier insisted that each urban district should have one and only one function. Statement II: Planning from above for large populations requires that the values being maximised are few and sharply specified, preferably a single one.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are Scott's claims, and II is exactly the logic Scott gives for functional segregation: a zone, road or forest with one purpose can be optimised, while a home with many uses cannot. The tempting 'does not explain' misses that Scott presents II as the reason behind I."},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Scott presents Brasília as a failure on every measure, including health and equality. Statement II: Scott argues that Brasília's plan made no concession to the desires, history and practices of its residents.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"Scott calls Brasília 'a rational, healthy, rather egalitarian, state-created city' and says its success was considerable if judged by its departure from older Brazilian cities, so I is false. II is his own wording; the failure he stresses is its inability to transform Brazil or make people love its way of life."},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims match Scott's presentation of Jane Jacobs? I: The order of a thing is determined by the purpose it serves, not by its surface regularity. II: Street safety depends mainly on professional surveillance by police and officials. III: Planners' aesthetic view of order drove them to the further error of segregating functions.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I and III are Jacobs as Scott presents her: she is a 'functionalist' about order, and the aesthetic error leads to single-use zoning. II is the opposite of her claim: the sidewalk peace is kept by 'an intricate, almost unconscious network of voluntary controls' among residents, not by police."},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"case","q":"A hypothetical new Dutch district gets an address system of sector codes that the municipality's GIS team calls perfectly logical. Residents, visitors and couriers keep getting lost, because every block looks the same and nobody can picture where anything is. Which idea from Scott fits this most precisely?","o":["Brasilite: a mock illness about missing the pleasures of street life","The death of the street: pedestrians separated from fast traffic","Utopian miniaturisation: a model city built from zero to teach a nation","Macro-order and micro-confusion: legible above, mystifying below"],"a":3,"e":"Scott, following Holston, notes that 'what seems like rationality and legibility' to administrators 'seems like mystifying disorder' to residents navigating identical sectors and acronyms. Brasilite is tempting, but it names the anonymity and lost pleasures of outdoor life, not disorientation produced by a legible system."},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"critique","q":"A rational choice theorist (HC6) reads Scott's account of Brasília. Which criticism of the high-modernist plan would this theorist most plausibly make?","o":["It ignored that workers and politicians follow their own incentives, so plan failure was predictable","It ignored the class relations of the capitalist land market, which it then reproduced at the city's edge","It was imposed without open, uncoerced dialogue among all the people it affected","It replaced slowly evolved general rules with one design, wiping out dispersed knowledge"],"a":0,"e":"Rational choice expects all actors, including state actors, to pursue their interests: candangos wanted land and title, and politicians courted them as a voting bloc, which is state failure in RC terms. The class-relations critique is Marxist, the dialogue critique Habermasian, and the evolved-rules critique Hayekian or evolutionary."},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase states Scott's view of Le Corbusier's straight lines and right angles most precisely?","o":["They were an aesthetic ideology that also gave a grid legible at a glance","They had no practical effect at all and were simply personal taste","They were costly, which is why Le Corbusier dropped them in Chandigarh","They followed from nature's own mathematical order, as Le Corbusier rightly claimed"],"a":0,"e":"Scott says the preference was 'no more, and no less, than an aesthetic ideology', yet adds that it 'provided a legible grid that could be easily grasped at a glance'. The 'no practical effect' option understates this, and in Chandigarh Le Corbusier actually replaced curves with rectilinear axes."},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"excerpt","q":"A municipal vision document argues: 'The new district will rise on cleared land as one integrated composition. Housing, offices and leisure each get their own zone. The scheme has been drawn up by experts, away from the noise of local politics and existing rules, so that it delivers the single correct solution.' Which reading grounds this, and why?","o":["Rydin's governmental model: politicians set the goals and planners select the means","Davoudi's 'expert on top': research leads policy with scientific inevitability","Scott's high modernism: clean slate, zoning by function and the Plan above politics","Alexander's cell (d): formal optimisation by one actor pursuing a discrete goal"],"a":2,"e":"Clearing the site, strict functional segregation and a plan that overrides politics and regulations match Le Corbusier's 'clean tablecloth' and 'The Plan: Dictator' as Scott describes them. The governmental model is the tempting distractor, but it keeps politicians in charge of goals, whereas this passage sidelines politics altogether."},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs how, according to Scott, the 'unplanned Brasília' came about?","o":["Workers were expected to leave; planners then built satellite towns for them at the edge, because the plan foresaw a large permanent service population","Workers were expected to leave; housing ran short, so they squatted and, using the 'candango' hero rhetoric, won land and title in competitive politics","Workers were expected to leave; they were moved into the superquadras, because the egalitarian plan gave every resident a similar flat","Workers were expected to leave; squatters were quickly removed, because a closed political system gave them no voice to protest"],"a":1,"e":"Scott says the workforce outgrew the Free City, squatted, used Kubitschek's praise of the candangos as heroes, and organised for land, services and title; a 'reasonably competitive political system' let them be heard. The satellite-town option fails because these settlements 'had never been anticipated' by the plan."},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"except","q":"Which statement is NOT consistent with Scott's account of Le Corbusier?","o":["His plans made no reference to local history or tastes and could be anywhere","His 'Plan: Dictator' placed a capricious autocrat at the top of the city hierarchy","He saw slums as both aesthetically disordered and an obstacle to efficient police work","He and CIAM wanted schoolchildren taught the principles of scientific housing"],"a":1,"e":"Scott stresses that at the apex sits 'not a capricious autocrat but rather a modern philosopher-king' applying scientific truth; the despot 'is the Plan'. The other three are in the chapter: placeless plans, the twofold objection to slums (aesthetics and policing), and CIAM's call to teach children scientific housing."},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"who","q":"Which course author would endorse this claim: 'States simplify complex social realities to make them readable from the centre, and large schemes built on such simplifications tend to miscarry because they ignore how people actually live'?","o":["Friedrich A. Hayek","James C. Scott","Simin Davoudi","E. R. Alexander"],"a":1,"e":"Legibility through simplification, and the miscarriage of schemes that ignore practice, is Scott's core argument (Brasília compared with scientific forestry). Hayek is the tempting option, but his chapter argues about democracy's inability to agree on detailed ends, not about state legibility and lived practice."}
);
NQ_3.push(
{"t":"scott","fmt":"contrast","q":"Scott writes that Brasília is to São Paulo or Rio as… ","o":["a city master plan is to a regional land-use vision","a planned suburb is to the historic inner city around it","a new town in Europe is to an older Latin American capital","scientific forestry is to the unplanned, natural forest"],"a":3,"e":"Both Brasília and scientific forestry are 'highly legible, planned simplifications' made to be monitored and directed from above, and both miscarry. The other pairs are not comparisons Scott makes and miss the point about legibility."},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"example","q":"Which feature of Brasília best illustrates Le Corbusier's principle of 'the death of the street'?","o":["Roads used only by motor traffic, with no streets as places to gather","Superquadra blocks that each had their own nursery and elementary school","Housing that was similar for all residents, differing only in size","A monumental axis defined by terraced embankments in an arc"],"a":0,"e":"Scott writes that Brasília has 'no streets in the sense of public gathering places; there are only roads and highways'. The schools and similar housing are part of the plan's social programme, not of the death of the street."},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"inverse","q":"Holston's term 'public visiting room', used by Scott, refers to…","o":["the lobby that each superquadra block shared among its residents","the traditional Brazilian square, open to all classes and many uses","the Plaza of the Three Powers as the new capital's main ceremonial space","the commercial centre planned for each group of four superquadras"],"a":1,"e":"The 'public visiting room' is the older Brazilian square: framed, accessible to all classes and used for processions, play, shopping and meeting. Brasília's Plaza of the Three Powers is the opposite: so vast that meeting a friend there would be like meeting in the Gobi desert."},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"not","q":"Which of these was NOT part of Brasília's plan as Scott describes it?","o":["About 25 square metres of green space per resident","Superquadra blocks with their own nursery and school","Similar housing for all, differing only in number of units","Lively mixed squares combining homes, shops and offices"],"a":3,"e":"Brasília separated residence, commerce and administration; Scott notes that the vitality of older squares depended on exactly that mix, which was designed out. The green space, superquadra schools and similar housing were all part of the plan's emancipating vision."},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"apply","q":"A municipality wants safer streets in a new district. Following Jacobs's argument as presented by Scott, what should it do?","o":["Add cameras and patrols, since order is kept by professionals","Separate homes from shops so residential streets stay quiet","Mix shops and homes so many people watch the street all day","Widen the streets so that traffic and pedestrians can be split"],"a":2,"e":"Jacobs's 'eyes on the street' need continuous use and people with a reason to watch, like the shopkeepers and window-watchers of Boston's North End. Patrols are the tempting answer, but she argues that social order is not brought about by policemen and officials."},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"define","q":"Scott calls the building of Brasília an attempt at 'utopian miniaturization'. What does this mean?","o":["Shrinking an existing capital city so that the state can run it more cheaply and efficiently","Creating a model of a new Brazil from zero, since remaking all of Brazil was inconceivable","Scaling the plan down to small superquadras so that residents can build a real community","Testing the new design ideas first on a detailed scale model before construction began"],"a":1,"e":"Kubitschek, despairing of a direct assault on all of Brazil, turned to 'creating from zero a utopian model' on state land whose new environment would transform its residents. The superquadra option confuses the block scale with the idea of a model city for a new nation."},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"contrast","q":"How does the starting point of Jacobs's critique differ from Le Corbusier's way of seeing the city, according to Scott?","o":["Le Corbusier saw the city from the air; Jacobs as a pedestrian on daily rounds","Le Corbusier relied on statistics; Jacobs on large surveys of resident opinion","Le Corbusier began with history; Jacobs began from a clean slate of principles","Le Corbusier designed for the poor; Jacobs designed mainly for the middle class"],"a":0,"e":"Scott stresses the 'unique perspective' of Jacobs: an ethnography of sidewalks and intersections, whereas Le Corbusier 'sees' his city first from the air. The statistics option is wrong for Jacobs, who criticised planners' statistical models for moving families like 'billiard balls'."},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"recall","q":"What was Le Corbusier's first act after taking over the planning of Chandigarh, according to Scott?","o":["He banned cars from the centre to create a pedestrian bazaar","He kept the curved plan but added housing near the capitol","He replaced central housing with an 'acropolis of monuments'","He built superquadra blocks modelled on those in Brasília"],"a":2,"e":"Scott uses this as evidence of functional segregation: housing planned for the centre was swapped for monuments on a 220-acre site far from homes. He also replaced the original curves with rectilinear axes, and the centre's hawkers were banned."},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"example","q":"Scott contrasts an army drawn up on the parade ground with an army in combat. What does the metaphor illustrate?","o":["Single-use zoning gives a tidy visual order, but of a city doing nothing","Mixed use creates confusion that only a strong central plan can control","Cities need a clear chain of command, just as armies do, in order to function well","Military planning shaped Le Corbusier's ideas about wide avenues"],"a":0,"e":"The parade-ground army has tidy lines but is 'an army doing nothing, an army on display'; a working city, like an army in combat, looks disorderly yet functions. The chain-of-command option reverses the point, since the metaphor criticises visual order."},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"recall","q":"According to Scott, how did Le Corbusier establish the needs of the people he planned for?","o":["Through detailed surveys among the slum dwellers of Paris and Algiers","By stipulating an abstract human subject with fixed material needs","By letting the market reveal what housing people would pay for","Through consultation with CIAM members and local governments"],"a":1,"e":"Scott writes that Le Corbusier stipulated 'an abstract, simplified human subject' needing so many square metres, so much air, light and services, and never admitted that residents had anything valuable to say. Surveys and consultation are exactly what he skipped."}
);
OQ_3.push(
{"t":"scott","fmt":"excerpt","pts":15,"q":"Excerpt from Scott (ch. 4): 'Virtually all the needs of Brasília's future residents were reflected in the plan. It is just that these needs were the same abstract, schematic needs that produced the formulas for Le Corbusier's plans. Although it was surely a rational, healthy, rather egalitarian, state-created city, its plans made not the slightest concession to the desires, history, and practices of its residents. In some important respects, Brasília is to São Paulo or Rio as scientific forestry is to the unplanned forest. Both plans are highly legible, planned simplifications devised to create an efficient order that can be monitored and directed from above.' (a) Which theoretical perspective does the author adopt? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a direct quote from the excerpt. (10)","model":"(a) A critique of (authoritarian) high-modernist planning: Scott's argument that state schemes built on legibility and simplification ignore local, practical knowledge and lived practice. On the knowledge dimensions it moves away from universal, expert-monopolised knowledge towards situated and pluralist knowledge.\n(b) Argument 1, legibility as simplification from above: the plan is called a 'highly legible, planned simplification' designed to create 'an efficient order that can be monitored and directed from above'. This is Scott's core concept: the state makes a complex reality readable from the centre, as in scientific forestry, and the comparison with the forest signals that the same logic is at work in cities.\nArgument 2, the abstract citizen versus situated practice: the needs planned for were 'abstract, schematic needs', and the plan made 'not the slightest concession to the desires, history, and practices of its residents'. The criticism is not that the plan was irrational or unhealthy (Scott concedes it was 'rational, healthy, rather egalitarian') but that universal expert knowledge replaced residents' own knowledge and practices, which is why the planned city miscarried (brasilite, the unplanned Brasília). A strong answer adds a contrast with the governmental model: the problem is not that experts analyse, but that a universal, legible order is imposed by a state whose plan makes 'not the slightest concession' to the people who live in it. The excerpt therefore reflects Scott's situated and pluralist view of who knows the city.","rubric":["Names Scott's critique of high modernism / state legibility (not 'rational planning' in general)","Argument on legibility and simplification from above, with a quote from the excerpt","Argument on abstract, schematic needs versus residents' practices or knowledge, with a quote","Explains how each quote shows the perspective, not only quoting","Notes that Scott does not deny the plan's rational or healthy qualities (nuance)"]},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"short","pts":5,"q":"Explain what Scott (following Holston) means by the irony of 'macro-order and micro-confusion' in Brasília, and why it matters for his critique of high modernism.","model":"Brasília was designed to be legible as a whole: uniform superquadras, sectors named by acronyms, a clear monumental axis. For administrators this looked rational and orderly. For residents navigating the city, the same uniformity produced 'mystifying disorder': few landmarks, blocks that all look alike, codes that only make sense from the centre. Holston notes that practical knowledge of the city 'actually decreases with the imposition of systematic rationality'. This matters for Scott because it shows that legibility for the state and order as experienced by people can diverge or even conflict, which is the same gap Jacobs identifies between visual and functional order.","rubric":["Legible order from above / for administrators","Disorientation for residents (identical blocks, acronyms, few landmarks)","Links to legibility as state simplification","Connects to the gap between visual and experienced order (Jacobs) or to Scott's wider critique"]},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"compare","pts":10,"q":"Both Scott (1998) and Davoudi (2006) criticise the faith in expert knowledge behind rational planning. Compare their critiques on (a) what goes wrong and (b) what kind of knowledge they want planning to value.","model":"(a) Scott's critique is external and political. High modernism combines a universalising science, a total plan and an authoritarian state that sweeps aside 'the elected authorities, the voting public, the constitution'. What goes wrong is that a legible, simplified order is imposed from above (Le Corbusier's 'The Plan: Dictator', Brasília on state land), producing brasilite and an unplanned Brasília that the planned city depends on. Davoudi's critique is internal to democratic policy-making. The problem is not authoritarianism but naive assumptions of an instrumental, linear model of research use: policy-making as rational, evidence only from positive science, experts value-free. In reality evidence competes with Weiss's four I's (ideology, interests, institutions, prior information) and policy is bricolage.\n(b) Both move away from monist, universal expert knowledge. Scott values the practical, local knowledge of residents (Jacobs's street-level order; mētis in the wider book). Davoudi widens evidence to include experiential and tacit as well as formal knowledge, but she remains committed to research: she wants an enlightenment model and an 'evidence-informed society', not less knowledge. So Scott stresses situated practice against the plan, Davoudi a two-way relation between research and policy.","rubric":["Scott: authoritarian state + total plan overriding politics; examples (Le Corbusier, Brasília)","Davoudi: linear/instrumental model and three misconceptions; evidence competes with four I's","Identifies Scott as external/political vs Davoudi as internal to democratic policy","Knowledge: Scott local practical knowledge; Davoudi plural evidence incl. tacit, still pro-research (enlightenment)","Uses concepts precisely and names both authors"]},
{"t":"scott","fmt":"position","pts":10,"q":"Place Scott's account of Brasília on the deliberate versus emergent dimension of the course framework. Is the city's outcome the product of deliberate design or of emergent processes? Justify with the text.","model":"Scott's account combines both poles, and that tension is his point. The intention was extremely deliberate: a 'utopian miniaturization' built from zero on land owned entirely by the state, with all contracts, licences and zoning in the hands of the planning agency, following CIAM doctrine and Le Corbusier's 'Plan: Dictator'. If any city could be the result of deliberate design, it was Brasília. Yet the outcome was largely emergent. Construction workers squatted, organised and won land and title; by 1980, 75 percent of the population lived in settlements 'that had never been anticipated'. The rich also built their own settlements. Scott concludes that 'the real Brasília, as opposed to the hypothetical Brasília in the planning documents, was greatly marked by resistance, subversion, and political calculation', and that the two Brasílias were 'symbiotic'. So Scott places high-modernist intention at the deliberate end but shows that real urban outcomes are emergent, shaped by many actors' practices. This supports his wider claim that order cannot simply be designed from above.","rubric":["Recognises deliberate intent (state land, total plan, utopian miniaturisation)","Shows emergent outcome (unplanned Brasília, 75% by 1980, resistance and politics)","Uses at least one quote or precise textual reference","Draws a conclusion about Scott's position (intended order vs emergent reality)"]}
);

// ===== davoudi =====
HQ_3.push(
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Davoudi presents the 'expert on tap' model as a non-linear alternative to the 'expert on top' model. Statement II: In Davoudi's account, the two instrumental variants differ only in the posited direction of influence between research and policy.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"Both variants belong to the instrumental model and are linear and direct; they 'differ only in the posited direction of influence', so II is true and I is false. The non-linear, indirect alternative is the enlightenment model, not the expert on tap."},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Davoudi argues that Geddes's 'survey-analysis-plan' was linear and instrumental, yet its plans were not really derived from scientific evidence. Statement II: In Geddesian planning the planner was a creative visionary who made an intuitive leap from the survey to the plan.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Davoudi notes that Geddesian evidence was only physical description and that the visionary planner leapt from survey to plan, so plans rested on intuition and aesthetic ideas of urban form. II is therefore the reason the linear method did not yield science-based plans; that shift came only with 1960s systems planning."},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"combo","q":"Which of the following belong to the three misconceptions that, according to Davoudi, underlie the instrumental view of evidence-based policy? I: Policy-making is a rational process. II: Evidence can only be generated through positive science. III: Research findings are mostly ignored by politicians.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"The three misconceptions are that policy-making is rational, that evidence comes only from positive science, and that experts are apolitical, value-free 'and they know best'. III is not a misconception but part of Davoudi's own real-world account (research sits on the shelf), so it cannot be one of the false assumptions."},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"case","q":"A hypothetical Dutch ministry needs to reach a housing target from the coalition agreement. It commissions a research institute for fast, usable answers on which instrument would work best; the institute answers only the questions put to it and sets no agenda of its own. The ministry has not yet chosen an instrument. In Davoudi's terms this is closest to…","o":["the political or tactical model, in which evidence is cherry-picked to justify a decision","the 'expert on tap' model, in which research follows policy concerns on demand","the enlightenment model, in which research slowly illuminates the wider landscape","the 'expert on top' model, in which research leads and policy follows the findings"],"a":1,"e":"Research is policy-driven, quick and usable on demand, which is the expert 'on tap, but not on top'. The tactical model is tempting, but it requires that the decision is already made and evidence is selected to legitimise it; here the choice of instrument is still open."},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"critique","q":"Which criticism of Davoudi's argument would a rational choice theorist (HC6) most plausibly make?","o":["She keeps research in a privileged position, although expert and lay knowledge claims can never be judged against each other at all","She overlooks that the state's use of evidence serves capital accumulation and the class interests of property owners","She still assumes some authority can gather and use knowledge, while relevant knowledge is dispersed and cannot be centralised","She treats 'interests' as one of four competitors, whereas self-interest of all actors should be the core explanation"],"a":3,"e":"Rational choice assumes all actors maximise self-interest, so it would treat Weiss's 'interests' (DEFRA, think tanks) as the basic mechanism rather than one factor among four. The capital-accumulation critique is Marxist and the dispersed-knowledge critique Hayekian; the claim that knowledge claims can never be judged is a relativist position the course explicitly distinguishes from situated knowledge."},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase of Davoudi's conclusion is most precise?","o":["Evidence should play a much smaller role in planning, since politics will always override research findings","Communicative process knowledge should replace evidence, because knowing how matters most","Less emphasis on evidence-based policy, more on an evidence-informed society and two-way links","Evidence-based policy can work once experts are made more independent from government"],"a":2,"e":"Davoudi asks for 'less emphasis on evidence-based policy' and more on 'an evidence-informed society', with policy and research agendas set through interaction. She does not want less evidence; and she criticises the 1980s and 1990s focus on process (knowing how) for neglecting substantive knowledge of cities (knowing what)."},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"excerpt","q":"A planning report argues: 'Facts do not speak for themselves. A set of figures only becomes evidence when it is used to support or refute a claim, and what counts depends on what is available at that time and place. The experience of practitioners and residents can serve as evidence as much as statistics can.' Which reading grounds this argument, and why?","o":["Rydin's governmental model, because knowledge is gathered positively to support decisions","Scott's account of high modernism, because local practical knowledge beats the expert plan","Davoudi's view of evidence: facts tied to propositions, tacit knowledge included","Davoudi's expert-on-tap model, because evidence is produced to meet policy demand"],"a":2,"e":"Davoudi widens Weiss's 'prior information' into a view of evidence: facts become evidence only when used to support a proposition, evidence includes expert and experiential, formal and tacit knowledge, and what counts is what is available and accessible. Scott is tempting because of local knowledge, but the passage is about what makes facts into evidence, not about plans overriding practice."},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs Davoudi's Green Belt example?","o":["Suburban owners' values sustain containment; Hall's 1973 study shows its effects are progressive; the policy persists because evidence and values agree","Suburban owners' values sustain containment; Hall's 1973 study shows its effects are regressive; the policy persists, so ideology outweighs evidence","Containment is left unresearched for decades; once Hall's 1973 study appears, policy makers reverse it; this shows the enlightenment model at work","Containment has to pass the soundness test; Hall's 1973 study fails that procedural test; so its evidence is excluded from the plan"],"a":1,"e":"Davoudi uses the Green Belt to illustrate Weiss's 'ideology': the policy was sustained by values even after Hall's 1973 study showed regressive effects, giving more to those who had most. The chain in which Hall finds progressive effects gets the study wrong, and the soundness test dates from the 2004 reform, not from the containment debate."},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"except","q":"Which statement is NOT consistent with Davoudi (2006)?","o":["The 1960s systems view drew on cybernetics, location theory and Popper's falsifiability","The 2004 soundness test used evidence in a quasi-judicial sense, as reasoned justification","The enlightenment model makes research benefits faster and more direct for policy makers","More information can make choices harder, which is why Keynes said governments hate it"],"a":2,"e":"The enlightenment model gives indirect, slower benefits: it 'illuminates the landscape' rather than producing punchy messages, so the claim of faster, direct benefits reverses it. The other statements are all in the article: systems planning's intellectual roots, the quasi-judicial soundness test and the Keynes quote."},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"who","q":"Which course author would endorse this claim: 'Policy-making is bricolage: ideas are borrowed, copied and cannibalised into ramshackle compromises, so a model of linear research use misdescribes it'?","o":["Le Corbusier, as presented by Scott","Davoudi, in her real-world account","Rydin, on the governmental model","Coase, as used in the HC6 lecture"],"a":1,"e":"Davoudi uses Ball's image of 'bricolage' to show that the real world of policy differs from the ideal of linear evidence use. Rydin's governmental model is the tempting distractor, but it describes the hope of rational public administration, in which knowledge flows positively into decisions."}
);
NQ_3.push(
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"apply","q":"A council keeps commissioning thick research reports that nobody reads, and officials want quick fixes instead of in-depth studies. Which of Weiss's four I's does this illustrate?","o":["Ideology: basic values that no data can change","Interests: evidence used to justify decisions","Institutional norms and practices of the organisation","Prior information: what is already known and available"],"a":2,"e":"Davoudi lists demand for quick fixes, report overload, a shortage of 'intelligent users' and politicians not reading long reports under institutions. Interests would require evidence to be used to legitimise an already-chosen decision, which is not the issue here."},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"not","q":"Following Taylor, Davoudi names shortcomings of Geddes's 'survey-analysis-plan'. Which was NOT one of them?","o":["It had no stage in which the problem was defined","It produced one single plan rather than alternatives","It stopped at the plan, without learning or updating","It relied too heavily on mathematical traffic models"],"a":3,"e":"The shortcomings were the missing problem definition, the single plan, and the process ending at the plan. Geddesian evidence was physical description; models of traffic and spatial interaction belong to the 1960s systems approach that came later."},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"define","q":"Davoudi cites Shulock's 'paradox of policy analysis'. What does it refer to?","o":["Analysts must stay neutral, yet they are hired by parties with clear political interests","Research is funded generously, yet the money is spent on topics nobody needs","Better analysis tends to produce more conflict instead of more agreement","The gap between how the policy process should work and its messy, political reality"],"a":3,"e":"The paradox is the mismatch between the rational ideal of policy-making and its 'messy, uncertain, unstable and political' reality, which Davoudi says both rational planning and today's evidence-based policy suffer from. The neutrality option sounds plausible but is closer to her third misconception than to Shulock's paradox."},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"example","q":"A House of Commons report found that 'no-one seems to have a clue what is going where' with hazardous waste. What point does Davoudi use this to make?","o":["Research for policy's sake is selective: little research exists where no policy problem was defined","Policy makers ignore research that conflicts with their ideology about environmental protection","Scientific uncertainty makes all evidence on environmental flows inconclusive and contested","Data on waste is held by private firms, so the state lacks the information it needs to plan effectively"],"a":0,"e":"Strategic waste planning had not been defined as a policy problem, so it attracted no research; this shows that policy-driven research is selective. The ideology option is tempting, but the example is about missing knowledge, not knowledge that was rejected."},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"recall","q":"With which approach does Davoudi associate the slogan 'What matters is what works'?","o":["The 1960s systems planning of McLoughlin and Chadwick","New Labour's evidence drive, the expert-on-tap variant","The enlightenment model of research and public debate","The RTPI's 2003 call for critical thinking about place"],"a":1,"e":"The slogan sums up the late-1990s British push for quick, usable evidence on demand, which is mostly the on-tap version; Davoudi notes it paid little attention to what works for whom and in what circumstances. The RTPI call is what she supports instead, as part of a broader enlightenment view."},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"inverse","q":"Which example does Davoudi use to show that evidence is often incomplete or contested while policy goes ahead anyway?","o":["DEFRA's definition of evidence as information that serves its priorities","The Cabinet Office audit finding that research was rarely used","The unresolved debate on high-density housing, already promoted","Charles Booth's 1887 social survey of poverty in London"],"a":2,"e":"Studies on the costs and benefits of high density stayed inconclusive and open to value-based readings, yet policy already treated high density as the route to sustainable communities. DEFRA illustrates 'interests', and the audit illustrates research sitting on the shelf."},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"contrast","q":"What does Davoudi criticise about British planning debates in the 1980s and 1990s?","o":["They focused on process (knowing how) and neglected substantive knowledge of cities","They relied so much on models that planners lost touch with residents' everyday experience","They let developers' think tanks decide which evidence would count in plans","They replaced evidence with intuition, returning to Geddes's visionary planner"],"a":0,"e":"Davoudi argues that the debate focused so much on process, for example communicative planning, that the knowing-what of how cities work was neglected; planning became a bureaucratic routine lacking imagination. The think-tank option mixes in her Thatcher-era point about 'interests', which is a different argument."},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"recall","q":"Why, according to Davoudi (quoting Keynes), might a government hate to be well-informed?","o":["Because information is expensive and research budgets are always limited","Because more information can make choices harder than relative ignorance","Because being informed creates a legal duty to act on every finding","Because experts tend to disagree, which undermines public trust in policy"],"a":1,"e":"Davoudi uses Keynes to show that more information does not automatically help: it can confuse choices that would be easier in relative ignorance. The cost and legal-duty options are not in the article; the expert-disagreement option is plausible but not her point here."},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"apply","q":"Which practice best fits Davoudi's enlightenment model of the research-policy link?","o":["A ministry orders a two-week study to back up a policy it announced in last month's budget","A long-running research programme feeds public debate on where and how cities should grow","A planning inspector checks whether each plan has an up-to-date and robust evidence base","A municipality buys a forecasting model that tells it exactly how many homes to plan"],"a":1,"e":"The enlightenment model is indirect and slow: research illuminates the context and informs public debate rather than delivering punchy answers. The inspector option describes the soundness test, which Davoudi found to be largely a procedural tick-box exercise."},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"recall","q":"According to Davoudi, what changed in the kind of evidence planning needed when 1960s systems and rational planning took over from Geddes?","o":["From analytical models to the physical survey of land and buildings","From expert reports to experiential knowledge gathered in consultation","From physical description to analytical evidence from theory and models","From national statistics to locally collected, qualitative case studies"],"a":2,"e":"Geddesian planning used descriptive, physical evidence; systems planning required analytical evidence drawn from location theory, logical positivism and Popper's falsifiable hypotheses, with computers and spatial interaction models. The option running from analytical models to physical survey reverses the direction of the change."}
);
OQ_3.push(
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"excerpt","pts":15,"q":"An article on regional housing strategy argues: 'Government has asked us to deliver robust evidence on housing need so that the plan can simply follow the numbers. But numbers alone have rarely settled where homes should go. In our region, protection of the green buffer has survived three studies questioning its effects, because residents and councillors value it. Our models are useful, but they sit next to what officials already know from practice, next to what the main landowners want, and next to the habits of a planning department that must produce results quickly. We therefore see our research less as a verdict than as a contribution to an informed public debate.' (a) Which theoretical perspective grounds this argument? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a direct quote from the passage. (10)","model":"(a) Davoudi's (2006) post-positivist critique of evidence-based policy: the passage rejects the instrumental, linear model of research use and endorses an enlightenment model of evidence-informed policy.\n(b) Argument 1, evidence competes with Weiss's four I's: the passage names all four. Ideology: the green buffer 'has survived three studies questioning its effects, because residents and councillors value it', which mirrors Davoudi's Green Belt example where values outweighed Hall's 1973 evidence. Interests: 'what the main landowners want'. Institutional norms and practices: 'the habits of a planning department that must produce results quickly' (the demand for quick fixes). Prior information: 'what officials already know from practice'. This shows that evidence does not enter a 'pristine environment'.\nArgument 2, rejection of the instrumental model in favour of enlightenment: the government's wish that 'the plan can simply follow the numbers' expresses the linear model of evidence-based policy and its misconception that policy-making is a rational process. The authors instead see their research 'less as a verdict than as a contribution to an informed public debate', which is the enlightenment model: research illuminates the landscape and informs debate, aiming at evidence-informed rather than evidence-based policy. A strong answer adds that this is not anti-research: Davoudi keeps a role for evidence but rejects the naive assumption that political processes can be 'technicized'.","rubric":["Identifies Davoudi / post-positivist critique of evidence-based policy (enlightenment vs instrumental)","Argument on Weiss's four I's (at least two named correctly) with a quote","Argument on rejecting linear/instrumental model and endorsing enlightenment/evidence-informed, with a quote","Links quotes explicitly to the concepts (e.g. green buffer = ideology, cf. Green Belt)","Notes that the perspective does not reject research as such"]},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"apply","pts":10,"q":"In the 22 Sept lecture, the social cost-benefit analysis (MKBA) of making the Westerscheldetunnel toll-free found a net welfare gain of about 27 to 39 million euros and benefits 1.5 times the costs, while noting that some effects, such as 'the feeling of injustice about the toll', could not be expressed in euros. Use Davoudi (2006) to analyse how such an MKBA should be understood in the decision.","model":"Seen through the instrumental model, the MKBA is evidence that should lead the decision: if benefits exceed costs, abolish the toll (expert on top). If the motion had already been adopted and the MKBA was commissioned to support it, the risk is the political or tactical model, where evidence legitimises a decision already made. Davoudi warns against both readings. Her three misconceptions apply: policy-making is not a purely rational process; the MKBA is only one kind of evidence; and the experts who built it made value-laden choices, such as how to price travel time and what to leave out. The excluded 'feeling of injustice' shows that experiential and tacit knowledge of the people of Zeeuws-Vlaanderen also counts as evidence in her widened sense, even if it cannot be expressed in euros. The decision will also be shaped by Weiss's four I's: ideology (regional fairness), interests (the province, the tunnel operator), institutional practices (the MIRT and MKBA rules) and prior information. Following Davoudi, the MKBA is best used in an enlightenment way: as one input that informs the political and public debate, not as a verdict. That is evidence-informed rather than evidence-based policy.","rubric":["Distinguishes instrumental (on top / on tap) or tactical use of the MKBA","Applies at least two of the three misconceptions to the MKBA","Treats non-monetised effects as experiential/tacit evidence","Uses Weiss's four I's or the enlightenment model to frame the decision","Concludes with evidence-informed use and justifies it"]},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"compare","pts":10,"q":"Compare Rydin's governmental model of rational public administration (2021) with Davoudi (2006) on the role and status of knowledge in planning decisions.","model":"In Rydin's governmental model, politicians set the ends and planners select the means; the planner 'brings expert knowledge to bear on the options available'. Knowledge is assumed to be gathered 'in a positive way, supporting decision-making', and the legitimacy of plans comes 'from the rationality of the analysis' put forward by planners. Knowledge is thus universal and largely monist: produced by experts, with techniques such as MKBA, and flowing into decisions. Davoudi questions exactly these assumptions. The enthusiasm for evidence-based policy rests on the same instrumental, linear view of research and on three misconceptions: policy-making is rational, evidence comes only from positive science, and experts are value-free and know best. In reality evidence competes with ideology, interests, institutional norms and prior information, policy is bricolage, and power shapes what counts as knowledge (Flyvbjerg). Her notion of evidence is pluralist and situated: experiential and tacit knowledge count, and what counts is what is available at a given time and place. Both keep a place for research, but where the governmental model sees it as the basis of legitimacy, Davoudi sees it as one contribution to a two-way, evidence-informed debate.","rubric":["Governmental model: ends/means split, positive knowledge, legitimacy from rational analysis","Davoudi: instrumental model + three misconceptions; four I's","Contrasts monist/universal with pluralist/situated view of knowledge","Notes common ground (both value research) and the difference in status","Accurate attribution to both authors"]},
{"t":"davoudi","fmt":"position","pts":10,"q":"Place Davoudi (2006) on the knowledge dimensions of the course framework (universal versus situated; monist versus pluralist; realist versus relativist). Justify each placement with the text.","model":"Universal versus situated: Davoudi leans towards situated knowledge. She argues that what counts as evidence depends on 'what is available and accessible' at a given time and place, and that new evidence must fit into policy makers' existing understanding; the slogan 'what matters is what works' ignores what works for whom and in what circumstances. Monist versus pluralist: she is pluralist about who can produce valid knowledge. Evidence includes formal and informal, expert and experiential, systematic and tacit knowledge, and she rejects the misconception that experts are value-free and 'know best'. Realist versus relativist: she is not a relativist. She still values research, quotes Popper that science 'does not rest upon solid bedrock' but keeps it as the best we have, and wants better use of evidence: an 'evidence-informed society'. Facts and claims can be judged, since validity concerns the link between facts and propositions. This matches the lecture's correction that situated does not mean 'anything goes'. Overall she moves away from the positivist, universal and monist assumptions of rational planning without abandoning a moderately realist commitment to research.","rubric":["Situated, with textual support (availability, for whom/circumstances)","Pluralist, with support (experiential/tacit; experts not value-free)","Not relativist / moderately realist, with support (keeps research, evidence-informed)","Uses the framework terms correctly (e.g. pluralist ≠ many survey respondents)"]}
);

// ===== alex =====
HQ_3.push(
{"t":"alex","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Alexander argues that Habermas's theory of communicative action (TCA) cannot serve as a general paradigm for planning. Statement II: TCA cannot account for pre-interactive deliberation: how an actor decides what course to pursue before entering the arena of discussion.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Alexander rejects TCA as a general paradigm because, among other reasons, it is limited to the social, interactive dimension and cannot say how individuals or units decide on their strategy beforehand. II is thus one of his reasons for I; it is also why he calls TCA the 'mirror image' of classic rationality."},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: In Alexander's contingency framework, the cell for real, individual, consensus-oriented action is empty. Statement II: Ideal theories cannot deal with the logical inconsistencies that characterise social interaction.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":1,"e":"Both statements are in the chapter, but they explain different empty cells. The real-individual-consensus cell is empty 'by definition', because consensus and understanding are properties of interaction, not of individual deliberation; II is the reason the other empty cell (ideal, individual, consensus) cannot be filled."},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"combo","q":"Which of the following does Alexander classify as an 'abandonment' response to the breakdown of rationality? I: Neo-Marxist substantive planning theory (Castells, Cooke). II: Simon's satisficing. III: Public choice theory, proposed by Poulton as a positive theory for planning.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"Substantive paradigms that replace the 'value neutral' process model, such as neo-Marxism and, as its counterblast, public choice theory, fall under abandonment. Satisficing substitutes a descriptive decision model for the rational paradigm, which Alexander classifies as 'avoidance'."},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"case","q":"A hypothetical consultancy builds a formal model of how three developers and a municipality will form coalitions over a large brownfield site, and simulates the moves each party is likely to make to secure its goals. Where does this activity belong in Alexander's contingency framework?","o":["Real, collective, accomplishment-oriented: strategic action such as bargaining and political games","Ideal, interactive, accomplishment-oriented: game theory and formal models of coalitions","Ideal, interactive, consensus-oriented: Habermas's communicative action aimed at shared understanding","Ideal, individual, accomplishment-oriented: optimisation of one actor's choice by linear programming"],"a":1,"e":"Formal game-theoretic and coalition models of interaction between goal-seeking actors belong in cell (e): ideal, social-interactive, accomplishment-oriented. The real strategic-action cell is tempting because the subject is bargaining, but a formal simulation is a normative or analytic model of the game, not an observed process."},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"critique","q":"A neo-Marxist planning theorist (one of the substantive 'abandonment' positions Alexander discusses) reads his contingency framework. Which criticism would this theorist most plausibly make?","o":["It sorts models by actor purpose and domain but ignores the class structure and capital interests that decide whose choices count","It still treats strategic bargaining as a legitimate domain, while only undistorted dialogue can produce valid planning norms","It assumes planners can deliberately design institutions, whereas institutions evolve through slow trial-and-error processes","It still allows that actors may sometimes seek consensus, although every actor, planners included, in fact always maximises self-interest"],"a":0,"e":"A neo-Marxist critique targets structure: the framework is procedural and leaves out capitalist relations and land interests, a point close to Healey's structural political economy approach that Alexander tries to fold into his framework. The undistorted-dialogue critique is Habermasian, the evolved-institutions critique is evolutionary (Hayek, Moroni), and the universal self-interest critique comes from rational choice."},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase states Alexander's view of the 'procedural versus substantive' debate most precisely?","o":["Substantive theory alone can be based on reality, so abstract procedural theory should be abandoned","Procedural and substantive theories are incompatible, so planners must choose one according to context","The debate is sterile: it is really about level of abstraction, and structural theory abstracts too","The debate is resolved by merging process and substance into one universal model at a single level"],"a":2,"e":"Alexander calls the debate 'essentially sterile' and says it has been 'mostly about something else: the appropriate level of abstraction'; generalising theories must abstract from cases whether they are procedural or structural. The claim that only substantive theory is based on reality is the position of Cooke and Poulton that he rejects; calling the two incompatible turns complementary concepts into a 'contrived conflict'."},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"excerpt","q":"A planning textbook argues: 'No single model of decision-making is right everywhere. Formal optimisation suits an organisation choosing internally among well-defined options; bargaining models describe arenas where several parties pursue their own goals; and the ideal of open dialogue applies only where participants truly seek agreement. The task of theory is to say which model fits which situation.' Which reading grounds this argument, and why?","o":["Lindblom's incrementalism, because planners should muddle through with limited comparisons","Habermas's communicative action, because valid decisions come from open and uncoerced dialogue","Rydin's governmental model, because planners pick the best means for politically given ends","Alexander's contingency framework, because each model is valid in its own domain of action"],"a":3,"e":"Assigning optimisation, strategic bargaining and communicative action to different domains by actor purpose and action domain is the core of Alexander's contingency framework: 'one paradigm cannot supplant another'. Habermas is tempting because dialogue is mentioned, but the passage limits dialogue to one domain rather than making it the paradigm."},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs Alexander's account of the intrinsic problem of contingency models?","o":["A contingency theory partitions the universe into situations; the contingencies are fixed by empirical data alone; so contingency models are fully objective and neutral","A contingency theory partitions the universe into situations; it can then only describe, not prescribe; so it cannot guide planning practice","A contingency theory partitions the universe into situations; the choice of contingencies is critical but arbitrary; so justifying it starts an infinite regress","A contingency theory partitions the universe into situations; contingencies are set by consensus among participants; so it collapses into TCA"],"a":2,"e":"Alexander admits that selecting the relevant contingencies is 'absolutely critical' yet 'essentially arbitrary', and any justification is 'the beginning of an infinite regress', so contingency models share the weakness of ideological models. He still claims his framework can prescribe as well as describe, which rules out the chain claiming it can only describe."},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"except","q":"Which statement is NOT consistent with Alexander's chapter?","o":["Rationality remains the normative basis for planners' claims to professional expertise","Most observed interpersonal and social interaction is strategic, not communicative","TCA is normative and entirely procedural, as divorced from substance as rationality","Earlier contingency models failed mainly because they left out bounded rationality"],"a":3,"e":"Earlier contingency models failed because they only integrated classic rationality with a few 'modified rationality' models (such as bounded rationality) and ignored critical theory's point about the social, interactive side of decisions. The other three statements are all claims Alexander makes."},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"who","q":"Which course author would endorse this claim: 'Classic rationality covers individual deliberation and communicative action covers interaction; neither can replace the other, so any new paradigm for planning must include both'?","o":["Friedrich A. Hayek","Simin Davoudi","Yvonne Rydin","E. R. Alexander"],"a":3,"e":"This is Alexander's 'mirror image' argument and his conclusion that 'one paradigm cannot supplant another'. Davoudi also relativises rationality, but she does so by criticising the linear model of evidence use, not by combining rationality with communicative action."}
);
NQ_3.push(
{"t":"alex","fmt":"define","q":"What does Alexander mean by rationality as a 'descriptive model'?","o":["A prescription telling planners how they ought to decide in every single case","A standard used to rank options by their utilitarian consequences alone","A way of explaining behaviour by asking whether actors act as if rational","A record of the steps planners actually took in a given planning process"],"a":2,"e":"In its descriptive role, we imagine that individuals and collectivities act as if they were rational and ask whether this makes sense of their observed behaviour. Prescription and analytic standards belong to rationality's other role, as a normative paradigm."},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"recall","q":"Which of Alexander's four responses to the breakdown of rationality offers radically different models, including contingency models such as Hudson's SITAR?","o":["The ritual response","The avoidance response","The abandonment response","The search response"],"a":3,"e":"The 'search' response proposes radically different alternatives; contingency models such as Hudson's SITAR, Lim's synthetic framework and Yiftachel's taxonomy belong here. Abandonment drops general process models for intuition or a substantive ideology instead."},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"inverse","q":"Which models does Alexander place in the real, individual, accomplishment-oriented cell of his framework?","o":["Optimisation, linear programming and multi-objective decision-making","Bounded rationality, satisficing, incrementalism and mixed scanning","Game theory and formal models of teams and of coalitions","Bargaining, negotiation, political games and strategic planning"],"a":1,"e":"Cell (a) holds the descriptive models developed in response to critiques of classic rationality. Optimisation and linear programming are ideal individual models (cell d), game theory is ideal interactive (cell e), and bargaining is real collective strategic action (cell b)."},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"define","q":"Critical theory adds the idea of 'reflexive consciousness'. In Alexander's summary of Habermas, what is good in a setting?","o":["What is traditional or customary in that particular community","What experts judge to be optimal after a full and careful rational analysis","How participants would act if communicating openly, without coercion","What a majority of affected people choose in a formal vote"],"a":2,"e":"For Habermas, what is good is 'not merely what is traditional or customary but how participants in that world would choose to act if they communicated openly and without coercion'. The expert-optimum option describes classic rationality, which critical theory challenges."},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"recall","q":"What is Habermas's 'universalization principle', as cited by Alexander?","o":["A norm is valid only if all affected parties accept it unconditionally and freely","A plan is valid only if it maximises the total sum of welfare for all affected people","A decision is valid only if experts can generalise it to all comparable cases","A rule is valid only if it is applied equally to every citizen without exception"],"a":0,"e":"Alexander uses this principle to show that TCA is general, universal and entirely procedural: its rules apply in any substantive context. The welfare-maximisation option describes the utilitarian logic of classic rationality instead."},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"not","q":"Which is NOT one of the three dimensions of Alexander's contingency framework (his figure of six filled cells)?","o":["Type of theory: ideal or real","Actor's purpose: accomplishment or consensus","Action domain: individual or collective","Level of abstraction: concrete or abstract"],"a":3,"e":"The level of abstraction (with the procedural-structural focus) is the pair of dimensions Alexander uses to dissolve the procedural versus substantive debate, not a dimension of the contingency framework. The framework's three dimensions are type of theory, actor's purpose and action domain."},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"contrast","q":"Why, according to Alexander, can Schön's 'reflective practitioner' not serve as a general normative base for decision and action?","o":["It rests on an essentially phenomenological, case-specific approach to action","It is a purely utilitarian model that ignores the interaction between actors","It assumes that all practitioners pursue their own self-interest strategically","It was designed for architects and cannot be applied to public planning"],"a":0,"e":"Alexander says models that 'rest on an essentially phenomenological approach to action' sit at the concrete end of the abstraction dimension and cannot generalise into a normative base. The utilitarian option describes classic rationality, the opposite of Schön's approach."},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"apply","q":"Where does Alexander place institutional design and coordinative planning in his framework?","o":["Ideal, individual, accomplishment-oriented, next to formal optimisation models","Real, collective, consensus-oriented, next to mediation and arbitration","Real, collective, accomplishment-oriented, as interaction among participants","Ideal, interactive, consensus-oriented, next to communicative action"],"a":2,"e":"Institutional design and coordinative planning involve interaction among participants or an 'implementation set' of organisations pursuing goals, so they fall in cell (b) with strategic action. Mediation sits in cell (c) only with neutral or consensus-oriented actors, which Alexander calls rare."},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"recall","q":"How does Alexander judge the prospects of his own contingency framework in the conclusion?","o":["It already replaces rationality as the accepted paradigm of the field","It is the only hope, perhaps no more than a hope, of an adaptable paradigm","It is a temporary tool until a general theory of planning is developed","It should be dropped as soon as practitioners prefer their own intuition to it"],"a":1,"e":"Alexander writes that replacing one simple paradigm by another is unlikely and that a contingency framework 'offers the only hope, perhaps no more than a hope' of a paradigm that can grow and adapt. He does not claim it is already accepted, and he doubts that a general theory of planning is possible at all."},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"contrast","q":"How does Alexander treat Healey's structural 'political economy' approach, which she contrasted with contingency approaches?","o":["As a false dichotomy: political economy is a subset of a wider contingency approach","As a superior alternative that should eventually replace his own decision-centred framework","As an abandonment response that must be rejected for its substantive ideology","As identical to Habermas's TCA, since both stress constrained communication"],"a":0,"e":"In a note, Alexander calls Healey's contrast 'a false dichotomy' and hopes to show that political economy is 'actually a subset' of a more general contingency approach; he also proposes her structural dimensions (state organisation, land interests, demand for land) as contingencies. He does not treat it as superior or as TCA."}
);
OQ_3.push(
{"t":"alex","fmt":"excerpt","pts":15,"q":"Excerpt from Alexander, 'After rationality: towards a contingency theory for planning' (punctuation lightly normalised): 'Two theories are juxtaposed within the contingency model. One is the classic theory of rational decision making and planning that is grounded in a utilitarian assessment of consequences. The other is the critical theory of communicative action. Both rationality and critical theory offer idealized prescriptions for choice. But rationality is limited to individual deliberation while critical theory deals only with communicative interaction. Each of these models is almost a mirror image of the other, focusing exactly on what its counterpart neglects. The complementary character of the two theories is the core of my claim that a contingency framework that combines them offers a promising way to provide an adequate account of planning.' (a) Which theoretical position does the author take in the rationality debate? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a direct quote from the excerpt. (10)","model":"(a) A contingency theory of planning: Alexander keeps rationality but relativises it. Neither classic rationality nor Habermas's communicative action can be the single paradigm; a framework should define the domain in which each is valid. This is the 'search' response to the breakdown of rationality.\n(b) Argument 1, complementarity instead of replacement: Alexander states that 'rationality is limited to individual deliberation while critical theory deals only with communicative interaction', and that each model is 'almost a mirror image of the other, focusing exactly on what its counterpart neglects'. Rationality ignores the collective, interactive side of action; TCA ignores the pre-interactive deliberation in which actors decide what to pursue. This leads to his conclusion elsewhere in the chapter that 'one paradigm cannot supplant another'.\nArgument 2, a contingency framework as the way forward: 'the complementary character of the two theories is the core of my claim that a contingency framework that combines them offers a promising way to provide an adequate account of planning'. Instead of one universal model, the framework sorts theories by type (ideal or real), actor's purpose (accomplishment or consensus) and action domain (individual or collective). Both theories are treated as 'idealized prescriptions for choice', so the framework also has to separate normative from descriptive models. A strong answer notes the modesty of the claim ('promising', and in the conclusion 'perhaps no more than a hope').","rubric":["Identifies contingency theory / Alexander's position in the rationality debate (not plain rational planning, not communicative planning)","Argument on complementarity / mirror image with a quote","Argument on the contingency framework combining both, with a quote","Explains deliberation vs interaction or normative vs descriptive","Shows how the quotes support the position"]},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"apply","pts":10,"q":"A hypothetical Dutch municipality plans a new solar park. (1) The planning department first ranks five sites internally with a multi-criteria analysis. (2) It then negotiates with two landowners and an energy cooperative, each pushing its own interests. (3) Finally, an independent mediator with no stake in the outcome leads sessions with residents who are committed to finding a shared solution. Use Alexander's contingency framework to analyse these three stages, and explain what the framework adds compared with relying on rational planning alone.","model":"Alexander's framework sorts decision models by type of theory (ideal or real), actor's purpose (accomplishment or consensus) and action domain (individual or collective). Stage 1 is individual deliberation by one unit acting as an individual towards a goal. The MCA itself is a formal tool of the ideal, individual, accomplishment-oriented cell (d), next to optimisation and multi-objective decision-making; how the department actually uses it, with limited time and information, is better described by cell (a): bounded rationality or satisficing. Stage 2 is real, collective and accomplishment-oriented: Habermas's strategic action in cell (b), with bargaining and negotiation, since each actor pursues its own goals. Stage 3 fits cell (c), real, collective and consensus-oriented, because the mediator is neutral and the participants seek agreement; Alexander stresses this is rare, and if the mediator worked for one party it would fall back into strategic action. What the framework adds: rational planning covers only stage 1 and ignores interaction, while communicative theory would idealise stages 2 and 3 and say nothing about stage 1. Since 'one paradigm cannot supplant another', the planner needs different models for different stages.","rubric":["Stage 1 placed in individual deliberation (cell d and/or a) with reason","Stage 2 placed in strategic action (cell b) with reason","Stage 3 placed in consensus-oriented real interaction (cell c), noting neutrality condition / rarity","Explains the added value: rationality ignores interaction, TCA ignores deliberation","Uses the three dimensions correctly"]},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"compare","pts":10,"q":"Compare Alexander's treatment of strategic action with the rational choice perspective presented in the HC6 lecture (Bouwmeester). Where do they agree, and where do they differ?","model":"Agreement: both take self-interested, goal-seeking behaviour as the realistic norm in planning. Alexander says 'it does not take an extreme degree of cynicism' to see that most observed interaction is strategic rather than communicative: actors use power, influence and manipulation to reach their own goals. His cell (b) contains bargaining, negotiation, political games and institutional design. Rational choice theory, as presented by Bouwmeester, likewise assumes that all actors, from citizens to developers and municipalities, maximise self-interest, and explains landowner strategies (framing, lobbying, negotiating at Rijnenburg) and Coasean bargaining in exactly these terms. Both also allow deliberate redesign of rules: institutional design for Alexander, changing property rights and transaction costs for rational choice. Differences: rational choice turns self-interest into a universal assumption and builds positivist, generalisable models (game theory, cost-benefit analysis) that cover the whole field; the public interest is only an aggregate of private interests. Alexander makes strategic action one domain among six. He keeps consensus-oriented action as a real (if rare) and ideal possibility, and keeps individual deliberation and normative models in separate cells. So for Alexander rational choice would be one partial model whose validity is contingent, not a general theory of planning.","rubric":["Agreement: strategic/self-interested action as dominant in practice, with Alexander's cell (b)","Agreement or link: deliberate design of rules / institutional design","Difference: RC universalises self-interest (all actors, positivist models, aggregate public interest)","Difference: Alexander keeps consensus-oriented and normative domains; RC is one contingent model","Accurate use of both sources"]},
{"t":"alex","fmt":"short","pts":5,"q":"Explain the two roles that rationality plays according to Alexander, and why this matters for any theory that wants to replace it.","model":"First, rationality is a descriptive model: we imagine that individuals and collectivities act as if they were rational and test whether this makes sense of their observed behaviour. Second, it is a normative paradigm, which works analytically (an abstract standard for analysing decisions, as in economics) and prescriptively (how we ought to decide). Despite its utilitarian bias, it remains the normative basis for planning methods and for planners' claims to professional expertise. This matters because Alexander argues that a successor paradigm, whether one theory or a set of related theories, must fulfil the same range of functions. Alternatives that only describe (satisficing) or only prescribe (TCA) fall short.","rubric":["Descriptive role explained (as-if rational, explaining behaviour)","Normative role explained (analytic and/or prescriptive)","Basis of planners' expertise mentioned","Implication: a successor must cover the same range of functions, with an example"]}
);

// ===== rc =====
HQ_3.push(
{"t":"rc","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Rational choice theorists expect Utrecht's 2018 short-stay rules (60 nights, registration number, fines) to be difficult to enforce. Statement II: Short-term rentals are hard to detect, and online platforms resist sharing their data with authorities.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"The lecture lists detection problems and platforms' resistance to data sharing as the reasons why such 'one-size-fits-all' rules face enforcement difficulties, so II explains I. Other listed problems (transaction costs of inspection, legal challenges, waterbed effects) add to it but do not make II irrelevant."},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Rational-comprehensive planning and rational choice theory both treat the state as a neutral optimiser of social welfare. Statement II: Rational choice theory looks at state failure as well as market failure.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"Only rational-comprehensive planning treats planning as value-free welfare optimisation by experts; rational choice assumes that all actors, state actors included, act on self-interest, which is why it considers state failure. So I is false and II is true; the shared word 'rational' is the trap."},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"combo","q":"Which of the following count as transaction costs in the sense used in the lecture on Coase? I: The difficulty of getting many affected parties to agree. II: The value a homeowner attaches to quiet. III: The cost of finding out who is affected and by how much.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"The multiple-parties problem and information costs are both on the lecture's list of transaction costs (with bargaining, enforcement and strategic behaviour). The homeowner's valuation of quiet (the 1,000 euros) is the size of the externality being bargained over, not a cost of bargaining."},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"case","q":"In a hypothetical Dutch polder, outside investors buy farmland next to a growing city at prices well above its agricultural value. They then lobby the province and frame the area as 'low-hanging fruit' to have it designated for housing. Which concept fits their behaviour most precisely?","o":["Rent-seeking: trying to capture the unearned value of land through the political process","The betterment/compensation asymmetry: owners keep planning gains yet claim compensation for losses","A regulatory taking: public regulation that lowers the value of privately owned property","Free-riding: profiting from a collective effort without contributing to its costs"],"a":0,"e":"Buying ahead of designation and lobbying to obtain it is an effort to capture rent, the unearned value of land, through the political process. The asymmetry is tempting because owners would keep the betterment, but it describes how gains and losses are distributed once planning acts, not the strategic pursuit of a designation."},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"critique","q":"Which criticism of the lecture's rational choice analysis of Airbnb would a Marxist-inspired scholar (HC4, e.g. Madden and Marcuse) most plausibly make?","o":["Regulators lack the dispersed local knowledge needed to set the right number of hosting permits for each street and block","Embedded norms of home and hospitality, not cost-benefit calculation, shape what hosts see as legitimate","Treating housing loss as an 'externality' to be priced hides that commodifying homes for profit is the problem","The analysis assumes one correct model, whereas policy is bricolage and evidence competes with ideology"],"a":2,"e":"A Marxist reading sees short-stay rental as the commodification of housing and a question of power and class, which matches the lecture's own critique that rational choice 'ignores power and inequality'. The dispersed-knowledge critique is Hayekian, the embedded-norms critique sociological institutionalist, and the bricolage critique comes from Davoudi."},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase states the point of Needham's 'Land-use planning will only succeed if it can influence private decisions' most precisely, as used in the lecture?","o":["Since owners have a de facto veto, planners should stop steering land use and leave it to the market","Since the state creates property rights, it can always oblige owners to develop as the plan says","Since owners are the end-beneficiaries of policy, planning succeeds by serving owners' interests first","Since owners have a de facto veto, a plan alone cannot make development happen without their decisions"],"a":3,"e":"The slide pairs the quote with the landowners' 'veto right': owners who do not want to develop can block a plan, so planning must work through private decisions. The option about leaving land use to the market overstates this; the option about owners as end-beneficiaries confuses roles, since owners are the target groups of policy while end-beneficiaries may be non-owners."},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"excerpt","q":"A policy brief argues: 'Rather than banning short-stay rentals outright, the city should issue a fixed number of hosting-night permits that hosts can trade among themselves, and let homeowners' associations negotiate house rules collectively instead of each neighbour complaining separately.' Which idea grounds this proposal, and why?","o":["Olson's logic of collective action, because large diffuse groups of neighbours free-ride","Coasean-inspired intervention, because it clarifies rights and lowers transaction costs","Standard land-use regulation, because it caps nights in residential zones to limit harm","Value capture, because it lets the city recover the betterment that hosts would keep"],"a":1,"e":"Tradable permits define a right that can be traded, and VvE negotiation replaces fragmented bargaining with collective bargaining; both are the lecture's Coasean-inspired tools. Olson is tempting because the brief deals with neighbours, but Olson explains why regulation lags, not how to design a bargaining solution."},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs Bouwmeester's rational choice hypothesis on Swiss densification?","o":["The farmland law caps farmland prices; farmers lose income and lobby for expansion; densification follows as a political compromise","The farmland law lets developers buy farmland cheaply; they prefer greenfield sites; municipalities densify to stop their expansion","The farmland law bars non-farmers from buying farmland; insiders capture betterment from greenfield rezoning; so they support expansion","The farmland law caps prices and bars outsiders; no outsider lobby pushes expansion; insiders gain scarcity rents and back densification"],"a":3,"e":"The BGBB caps transfers at 5 to 15 percent above agricultural value and bars non-farmers, so there is no speculative outsider lobby for expansion, while insider owners profit from urban scarcity rents when growth is directed inward. The chain in which insiders support expansion is tempting but wrong: the price cap keeps speculative gains on farmland out, so expansion is not where insiders' rents lie."},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"except","q":"Which statement is NOT consistent with the HC6 lecture?","o":["Taylor shows that both keeping growth limits and avoiding growth controls can be rational rent-seeking","Rational choice can explain reproduction (insiders protect interests) as well as transformation","When transaction costs are high, Coase implies planners should fall back on uniform blanket bans","Self-interest in rational choice is not only economic, and it applies to municipalities as well"],"a":2,"e":"The lecture argues the opposite: when transaction costs are not zero, planners can still design Coasean-inspired interventions that clarify rights and enable negotiation, instead of one-size-fits-all restrictions that are hard to enforce. The other statements match the slides on Taylor, on reproduction and transformation, and on core assumptions."},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"who","q":"Which author, as quoted in the HC6 lecture, would endorse this claim: 'Property rights can be deliberately changed in order purposefully to influence land use'?","o":["Mancur Olson","Barrie Needham","David Ricardo","Nicole Gurran et al."],"a":1,"e":"The slide quotes Needham (p. 28) to show that rational choice can explain transformation: rules and rights can be redesigned to shape incentives. Olson explains why large groups fail to organise, Ricardo supplies the definition of rent, and Gurran et al. frame externalities managed 'through regulation or taxation'."}
);
NQ_3.push(
{"t":"rc","fmt":"not","q":"Buitelaar et al. (2025), shown in the lecture, identify landowner strategies in Rijnenburg. Which was NOT one of them?","o":["Framing the area as 'low-hanging fruit'","Collaborating in a consortium of developers","Lobbying and negotiating with the government","Taking the municipality to court for damages"],"a":3,"e":"The four strategies were framing, collaborating, lobbying and negotiating. Litigation for planning damages relates to the separate topic of regulatory takings and compensation, not to this list."},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"define","q":"In the lecture's list of problems with one-size-fits-all Airbnb rules, what is a 'waterbed effect'?","o":["Regulation pushes the problem elsewhere, such as other areas or platforms","Rising rents from tourism push long-term tenants out of the historic inner city","Strict rules make hosts organise into a stronger and better-funded lobby","Fines are so low that hosts treat them as a normal cost of business"],"a":0,"e":"A waterbed effect is an unintended outcome: pressing down the problem in one place makes it pop up elsewhere. Rising rents for tenants is one of the externalities that regulation is meant to address, not a side effect of the regulation itself."},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"recall","q":"On the lecture's Coase graph, where does bargaining move the level of pollution, whoever holds the initial right?","o":["To zero, because victims can always buy out all emissions","To X2, the level an unrestricted polluter would choose","To the level set by the planner's emission standard","To X1, where marginal benefit equals marginal damage"],"a":3,"e":"Bargaining leads to X1, where the polluter's marginal benefit equals the victims' marginal disbenefit; this is the efficient outcome regardless of who holds the right. X2 is the polluter's choice without bargaining, and zero emissions would forgo benefits worth more than the damage."},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"inverse","q":"The lecture (after Needham) describes a 'dual role' of the state in planning. What are its two roles?","o":["It creates and protects private property rights, and makes public policies aimed at owners","It plans land use, and it develops land itself through its own development company","It sets the goals of planning, and it hands over all of the implementation to private market actors","It regulates the market, and it compensates owners for all value lost to planning"],"a":0,"e":"The state creates and protects property rights, which serve owners, and makes public policies whose target groups are often those owners while end-beneficiaries may be non-owners. This dual role is why landowners hold a de facto veto that limits land-use planning."},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"recall","q":"How does the lecture, following Rydin (2020), define rationality in rational choice theory?","o":["Choosing the option experts judge optimal for society as a whole","Weighing costs and benefits of choices, as actors driven by their interests","Following agreed procedures so that every decision can be justified to others","Acting on long-held norms and habits that make choices predictable"],"a":1,"e":"Rationality is decision-making in which costs and benefits are weighed by actors driven by their own interests. Expert optimisation describes rational-comprehensive planning, and justification to others is the 'giving reasons' part of Alexander's definition."},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"example","q":"In the lecture's map of Airbnb incentives, which group bears the externalities of short-term rental?","o":["Professional hosts, through new tax burdens","Guests, through higher and less flexible prices","Neighbours, through noise and housing pressure","Platforms, through rising costs of monitoring"],"a":2,"e":"Neighbours face noise, disruption, weaker community ties, effects on their property values and pressure on housing supply: costs that do not show up in the host's price. Hosts gain a new income stream and guests get cheaper, more flexible stays."},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"apply","q":"Why does the lecture list negotiations within a homeowners' association (VvE) as a Coasean-inspired tool for Airbnb?","o":["Because the VvE can ban all rentals and so remove the need for any public rules","Because it turns many fragmented negotiations into one collective bargaining process","Because the VvE gives the municipality data on every host in the building complex","Because it shifts the full costs of enforcement onto the online platforms and their users"],"a":1,"e":"Collective bargaining within the VvE lowers transaction costs, especially the multiple-parties problem, so residents and hosts can reach an agreement themselves. Getting platform data is a different tool, digital monitoring, which addresses enforcement."},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"recall","q":"The lecture's figure of planning phases (after Bonczek and Halstenberg) shows how land value develops before construction. What does it show?","o":["Value stays flat until the building permit is granted, then jumps at once","Value rises with each planning step, the biggest jump being at zoning","Value falls when intentions are published, due to uncertainty","Value peaks at servicing and then drops once fees are paid"],"a":1,"e":"Every planning decision adds value, from published intentions to the building permit, with the largest jump at zoning; this is why owners buy and lobby early. In the Swiss version of the figure, the farmland law keeps value at agricultural level until zoning."},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"example","q":"What happened in the Rijnenburg case (Utrecht), as presented in the lecture?","o":["Residents organised and blocked all housing through a referendum","The municipality bought all the land and developed it without any developers","After 2008 developers' plans stalled and an energy-landscape vision arose","The province rezoned the area for housing at the developers' request"],"a":2,"e":"Before 2008 developers negotiated with the municipality about housing; the financial crisis made their business case unviable and an alternative vision of Rijnenburg as an energy landscape emerged. The lecture then used the case to show landowner strategies such as framing and lobbying."},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"contrast","q":"In the lecture, Taylor (2016) analyses the 'rational behaviour' of two kinds of existing owners. Which pair is it?","o":["Owners who sell early, and owners who hold land until rezoning","Owners who develop themselves, and owners who lease to developers","Owners who want growth limits kept, and owners who avoid controls","Owners who join a consortium, and owners who negotiate on their own"],"a":2,"e":"Taylor distinguishes owners who aim to preserve restrictions on growth and owners who try to avoid controls limiting future development; both are rational from a rent-seeking perspective. The consortium option mixes in the Rijnenburg strategies of Buitelaar et al."}
);
OQ_3.push(
{"t":"rc","fmt":"short","pts":5,"q":"Explain what the lecture calls the 'big asymmetry' between planning gains and planning losses, and why rational choice theory expects landowners to behave this way.","model":"When planning raises land values, through rezoning or infrastructure, landowners often keep the gain (betterment). When planning lowers values, through downzoning, heritage or conservation status or infrastructure decisions (regulatory takings), they often claim compensation, and such planning damage is often compensated. Rational choice expects this because owners act rationally to capture rent, defined with Ricardo as income from land above the cost of production. Keeping gains and passing on losses is the self-interested strategy for each owner. Value capture mechanisms try to correct the asymmetry; Dutch planschade compensation without a matching betterment levy is a close example.","rubric":["Betterment kept when values rise","Compensation claimed when values fall (regulatory takings)","Rent / rent-seeking as the explanation (self-interest)","Mentions value capture or an example"]},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"apply","pts":10,"q":"A hypothetical wind turbine is planned near a Dutch village. About 300 households fear noise and shadow flicker; the developer expects a large profit. Analyse this situation with Coase's theorem as presented in the lecture, and propose two Coasean-inspired interventions.","model":"The noise and flicker are externalities: costs imposed on households that do not appear in the developer's price. Coase's theorem says that if property rights are clearly defined and transaction costs are zero, bargaining between developer and residents leads to the efficient outcome whoever holds the right; only who pays whom changes, as in the noisy factory example. If the turbine's profit exceeds the residents' damage, it is built with compensation (or paid-for mitigation); if not, it is not built or is moved. In reality transaction costs are high. With 300 households there is a multiple-parties problem and room for holdouts; information costs arise because damage per household is uncertain; and bargaining and enforcement are costly. Rational choice warns that a one-size-fits-all rule (a fixed distance norm) may be hard to enforce and may be challenged. Coasean-inspired interventions: (1) clarify rights, for example a clear standard for acceptable noise and flicker that residents hold and the developer can buy down; (2) lower transaction costs through collective bargaining, for instance a village association negotiating once on behalf of all households, with payments into a local fund (comparable to the hosting tax into a neighbourhood fund for Airbnb). A strong answer notes the lecture's critique that this ignores power and inequality between a developer and residents.","rubric":["Identifies externality and states Coase's two conditions","Explains that the outcome is efficient whoever holds the right (who pays whom)","Names relevant transaction costs (many parties, holdouts, information, enforcement)","Two Coasean interventions that clarify rights and/or lower transaction costs","Mentions a limitation or critique"]},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"excerpt","pts":15,"q":"An article on urban growth argues: 'Why do some regions densify while others keep expanding? We expect landowners to support whatever strategy maximises their returns. Where outside developers can buy farmland cheaply and hope for rezoning, they form a powerful lobby for expansion. Where the law keeps farmland at agricultural value and closes it to outsiders, that lobby disappears, and owners inside the city gain more from scarcity when growth is directed inward. Change the property rules, and the incentives change with them.' (a) Which theoretical perspective grounds this argument? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a direct quote from the passage. (10)","model":"(a) Rational choice theory (as in the HC6 lecture; the argument closely follows Bouwmeester's hypothesis on Swiss densification).\n(b) Argument 1, self-interested actors and rent-seeking: the passage assumes that actors weigh costs and benefits to pursue their own interests: 'We expect landowners to support whatever strategy maximises their returns.' Developers who 'buy farmland cheaply and hope for rezoning' are seeking rent, the unearned value of land that planning decisions create, and they organise as 'a powerful lobby for expansion'. The insiders' support for densification is equally self-interested: they 'gain more from scarcity when growth is directed inward'. Behaviour is explained by incentives, not by values or structure.\nArgument 2, property rights and institutions shape incentives and can be deliberately changed: 'Change the property rules, and the incentives change with them.' This is the rational choice assumption that property rights and other institutions shape actors' strategies, echoing Needham: 'property rights can be deliberately changed in order purposefully to influence land use'. The law that 'keeps farmland at agricultural value and closes it to outsiders' corresponds to the Swiss BGBB. The passage also shows that the approach yields a testable hypothesis about a specific relationship, which the lecture recommended (thesis tip). A strong answer notes that this explains both reproduction (insiders protecting interests) and transformation (deliberately redesigning rules), and the typical critiques (ignores power, too agency-focused).","rubric":["Names rational choice theory","Argument on self-interest / cost-benefit / rent-seeking with a quote","Argument on property rights or institutions shaping incentives (deliberately changeable) with a quote","Links to Swiss/BGBB or Needham correctly","Explains the link between quotes and perspective"]},
{"t":"rc","fmt":"commentary","pts":15,"q":"An article on short-stay rentals argues: 'Bans on short-term rental are costly to enforce and push the problem elsewhere. A better solution is a market in hosting-night permits: the city fixes the total number of nights, hosts buy and sell permits, and part of the revenue goes to a neighbourhood fund. Those who value hosting most will end up with the permits, and neighbours are compensated.' (a) Which theoretical perspective grounds this argument? (b) Critique it from an alternative perspective of your choice. (c) What would that alternative perspective recommend instead?","model":"(a) Rational choice theory, specifically a Coasean-inspired intervention. The argument treats short-stay rental as an externality problem, criticises one-size-fits-all bans for enforcement difficulties and waterbed effects, and proposes to define a tradable right (hosting nights) so that bargaining allocates it to those who value it most, with a neighbourhood fund as compensation. This follows the lecture's tools: tradable permits and hosting taxes into a neighbourhood fund, aimed at efficiency judged in costs and benefits.\n(b) Critique from a Marxist / critical political economy perspective (e.g. Madden and Marcuse). The analysis treats housing as a commodity whose 'externalities' can be priced, while the core problem is the commodification of homes: dwellings are converted from use value for residents to exchange value for investors. 'Those who value hosting most' are those with most capital, typically professional hosts and investors, so a permit market would concentrate rights in their hands; compensating neighbours with a fund does not return housing to residents. This echoes the lecture's own critiques that rational choice ignores power and inequality and uses an oversimplified model of human behaviour. It also takes the existing distribution of property rights as given.\n(c) A Marxist-inspired planner would recommend decommodifying housing rather than creating a new market: strict limits or bans on converting homes to tourist use, priority for permanent residents, expanding social and non-market housing, and strengthening tenants' organisation, so that housing is treated as a home and not an asset.","rubric":["(a) Rational choice / Coasean: names tradable rights, transaction costs or efficiency","(b) Coherent alternative perspective named and applied (e.g. Marxist: commodification, power, inequality)","(b) Critique engages the specific argument (permits go to those with capital; compensation ≠ housing)","(c) Recommendation follows from the alternative's logic","Uses course concepts and authors accurately"]}
);
